{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 45, :comments ["<b>And Pharaoh called Tzafenat Paneaḥ.</b> It is the custom to change a person's name when he rises to greatness." "<b>And he gave him Osnat daughter of Poti Fera.</b> This is Potiphar, as Rashi explained. And Joseph took her as a wife, for if he were to take another, even one greater than she, he feared lest Potiphar say: they are my slaves, for I bought their father for myself as a slave; but when he married his daughter, he would not embarrass him or his household. Another interpretation: he took her in order to publicize that he did not lie with her mother. And some say: because she grew up in his house she is called his daughter, as we find with Moses our master, as it is written ״these are the sons of Bitya daughter of Pharaoh״ — because she raised him he is called by her name. As it is in the midrash: she was the daughter of Dinah from Shechem, and our father Jacob's sons sought to kill her. Our father Jacob came and hung an amulet on her neck and banished her from his house, and she hid herself among the thorns and the weeds, and on account of the bush (sneh) she was called Osnat. And Gavriel came and brought her to Egypt before Potiphar's wife, and she raised her. And when Joseph came to Egypt, all the women went out to see him because of his beauty, as it is written ״daughter of tza'adah upon the wall,״ and each one threw him an object or ornament, and this one had nothing to throw, so she threw him the amulet from her neck. And Joseph discerned in it that she was the daughter of our father Jacob's daughter, and he married her. \nAnother interpretation: she was the daughter of Dinah from Shechem, and our father Jacob's sons sought to kill her. Our father Jacob came and hung an amulet on her neck and sent her away from his house, and she hid herself among the thorns and the brush, and because of the bush (sneh) she was called Osnat. Gavriel came and brought her to Egypt to the house of Potiphar's wife, and she raised her. When Joseph came to Egypt, all the women went out to see him on account of his beauty, as it is written ״the daughters of tza'adah stepped upon the wall,״ and each one would throw him some object or ornament, but this one had nothing to throw, so she threw him the amulet that was on her neck. Joseph examined it and saw that she was the daughter of Jacob's daughter, and he took her as a wife." "<b>Poti Fera.</b> Rashi explained: this is Potiphar — because he was castrated; פרע (fera) is a term of פריעה (exposing/uncovering). There is castration by the sun (saris ḥammah) — one who is unable to have relations with a male but only with a female, and Potiphar was a saris ḥammah, for if you do not say so, where would he get a daughter from? And furthermore, is it not written ״according to these words did he do to me your servant״?"], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {1 "The source text contains duplicated material — the midrash about Dinah's daughter appears to be written twice in succession with minor variations. Both versions are translated faithfully in order, separated by a line break, to reflect the text as it stands. The duplication may originate from the compiler drawing on multiple midrashic sources without consolidation."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:08:26.459265711Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}